1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,612
So where are we, actually?

2
00:00:01,612 --> 00:00:08,200
The honest answer is: further than the headlines suggest, and much further from the finish line than the headlines imply.

3
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:12,287
There are now five or six serious approaches, and none of them has won.

4
00:00:12,287 --> 00:00:15,100
Superconducting circuits are the most mature.

5
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:18,525
Google, IBM, and a cluster of startups use them.

6
00:00:18,525 --> 00:00:25,212
They are fast, with gates in nanoseconds, and they are manufactured with the tools of the semiconductor industry.

7
00:00:25,212 --> 00:00:27,150
Their weakness is wiring.

8
00:00:27,150 --> 00:00:34,525
Every qubit needs control lines, and at a million qubits the wiring problem becomes a manufacturing problem of its own.

9
00:00:34,525 --> 00:00:40,400
IBM's answer is to move the control electronics into the fridge, stacked in layers above the chip.

10
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:42,487
Trapped ions are the most precise.

11
00:00:42,487 --> 00:00:48,962
Quantinuum's machines hold a handful of ions in an electromagnetic trap and manipulate them with lasers.

12
00:00:48,962 --> 00:00:56,900
Their gate fidelities are the best in the world, above ninety-nine point nine percent, in some cases above ninety-nine point nine nine.

13
00:00:56,900 --> 00:00:58,525
Their weakness is speed.

14
00:00:58,525 --> 00:01:01,575
Gates take microseconds, not nanoseconds.

15
00:01:01,575 --> 00:01:07,312
And scaling means shuttling ions around a trap, which is delicate, brilliant, and slow.

16
00:01:07,312 --> 00:01:10,075
Neutral atoms are the fastest-moving field.

17
00:01:10,075 --> 00:01:19,962
Instead of ions, you trap neutral atoms in arrays of laser light, optical tweezers, and you can arrange hundreds or thousands of them in arbitrary patterns.

18
00:01:19,962 --> 00:01:28,137
In twenty twenty-three, a Harvard-led team used this approach to demonstrate forty-eight logical qubits, and the numbers have been climbing since.

19
00:01:28,137 --> 00:01:35,362
The weakness is that neutral-atom gates are slower and less precise than ion traps, and the lasers are power-hungry.

20
00:01:35,362 --> 00:01:37,225
Photonic machines use light.

21
00:01:37,225 --> 00:01:43,100
They are attractive because photons don't decohere easily and they can travel down optical fibre.

22
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:51,862
PsiQuantum is trying to build a fault-tolerant machine with silicon photonics, and has said it aims for a utility-scale system before the end of the decade.

23
00:01:51,862 --> 00:01:59,250
The weakness is that photons don't interact with each other easily, which makes two-qubit gates hard, and loss is brutal.

24
00:01:59,250 --> 00:02:03,162
And then there is Microsoft, which is trying something entirely different.

25
00:02:03,162 --> 00:02:08,237
In February twenty twenty-five, Microsoft announced a chip called Majorana 1.

26
00:02:08,237 --> 00:02:10,887
It was not a general-purpose quantum processor.

27
00:02:10,887 --> 00:02:16,575
It was a demonstration of a new kind of qubit, based on a particle called a Majorana fermion.

28
00:02:16,575 --> 00:02:23,387
A particle that is its own antiparticle, predicted in nineteen thirty-seven, never definitively observed.

29
00:02:23,387 --> 00:02:25,737
Microsoft's claim was extraordinary.

30
00:02:25,737 --> 00:02:32,087
Topological qubits are protected by the topology of the material itself, not by careful control.

31
00:02:32,087 --> 00:02:34,900
They should be far more stable than any other kind.

32
00:02:34,900 --> 00:02:39,212
If they work, they could skip the error-correction problem almost entirely.

33
00:02:39,212 --> 00:02:44,787
The announcement was met with excitement and deep scepticism, in roughly equal measure.

34
00:02:44,787 --> 00:02:52,000
The company published a paper in Nature describing the physics, but the paper did not demonstrate a working topological qubit.

35
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,925
It demonstrated evidence for the material platform.

36
00:02:54,925 --> 00:03:02,925
Independent physicists pointed out that the central signature, the presence of Majorana modes, has been claimed before and retracted.

37
00:03:02,925 --> 00:03:08,837
Microsoft itself had a paper retracted in twenty eighteen after the data could not be reproduced.

38
00:03:08,837 --> 00:03:09,775
Is it real?

39
00:03:09,775 --> 00:03:12,275
The honest answer is: not yet proven.

40
00:03:12,275 --> 00:03:16,037
The company says it will have a scalable machine by the end of the decade.

41
00:03:16,037 --> 00:03:20,150
The physics community is watching, and waiting, and not convinced.

42
00:03:20,150 --> 00:03:21,562
That is not cynicism.

43
00:03:21,562 --> 00:03:23,125
It is how the field works.

44
00:03:23,125 --> 00:03:29,112
Quantum computing has a long history of beautiful claims that did not survive contact with a lab.

45
00:03:29,112 --> 00:03:33,387
The same month, Amazon announced a different approach: cat qubits.

46
00:03:33,387 --> 00:03:40,987
A cat qubit is a superconducting circuit designed so that one kind of error, bit flips, is exponentially suppressed.

47
00:03:40,987 --> 00:03:45,850
Amazon claimed a prototype with up to ninety percent reduction in that error channel.

48
00:03:45,850 --> 00:03:50,675
It is a real result, on a small device, and it addresses one half of the problem.

49
00:03:50,675 --> 00:03:53,675
The other half, phase errors, remains.

50
00:03:53,675 --> 00:03:55,737
So the race is not a single race.

51
00:03:55,737 --> 00:04:00,162
It is a set of parallel bets on physics, and nobody knows which one pays.

52
00:04:00,162 --> 00:04:02,575
What is not in doubt is the consequence.

53
00:04:02,690 --> 00:04:12,465
In August twenty twenty-four, NIST finalised the first post-quantum encryption standards: three algorithms designed to resist an attack by a quantum computer.

54
00:04:12,465 --> 00:04:16,215
ML-KEM, ML-DSA, SLH-DSA.

55
00:04:16,215 --> 00:04:20,677
They are already being deployed in browsers, phones, and secure messaging.

56
00:04:20,677 --> 00:04:24,565
The reason is not that quantum computers can break encryption today.

57
00:04:24,565 --> 00:04:25,540
They cannot.

58
00:04:25,540 --> 00:04:28,415
The reason is harvest now, decrypt later.

59
00:04:28,415 --> 00:04:32,565
An adversary can record encrypted traffic today and wait.

60
00:04:32,565 --> 00:04:38,627
If a large quantum computer arrives in fifteen years, everything recorded now becomes readable.

61
00:04:38,627 --> 00:04:44,990
That is a strange kind of threat, because it is the first cryptographic threat that is entirely about the future.

62
00:04:44,990 --> 00:04:47,102
It assumes the machine will exist.

63
00:04:47,102 --> 00:04:49,627
It prepares for the possibility that it won't.

64
00:04:49,627 --> 00:04:53,677
And that, in a way, is the whole story of quantum computing.

65
00:04:53,677 --> 00:04:58,665
We are building a technology on a bet about physics that we cannot yet fully verify.

